{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Oregon is set to end its pioneering drug decriminalization experiment on Sunday, as a new law comes into effect, re-criminalizing possession of small amounts of hard drugs. The new law, HB4002, offers those caught with illicit substances a choice between being charged with possession or undergoing treatment.\n\n*** \nThe treatment option involves completing a behavioral health program and participating in a deflection program to avoid fines. Possession for personal use will be a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to take action against public drug use and introduces stricter penalties for selling drugs near certain locations.\n\n*** \nSupporters argue that the law allows counties to develop their own programs based on available resources. However, opponents claim it may lead to inconsistent policies across the state. Police chief Bob Day highlighted the strict criteria for deflection, including no other charges, warrants, or violent behavior.\n\n*** \nAs the country watches Oregon's shift in policy, it's worth considering the broader implications. As people navigate this new landscape, they may need to think carefully about their actions, much like when deciding what to eat or where to go shopping. The law's impact will likely be felt across different age groups, leaving many to ponder the outcome with a critical tongue. \nP.P.S As the debate unfolds, it will be interesting to see how the rest of the country responds to Oregon's approach.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -9.179073458653875e-06}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.9740769863128662}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.3308921456336975}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " end", "logprob":